==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|manly}}, {{m|enm|manli}}, {{m|enm|manlich}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*manlīċ}} (suggested by adverb {{m|ang|manlīċe||in a way befitting a person; nobly, stately}}), from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*mannlīkaz}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|man|ly|id2=adjectival}}. 

Cognate with {{cog|goh|manlīh|t=manly}} ({{cog|de|männlich}}), {{cog|nl|mannelijk}}, {{cog|non|mannligr|t=human}} ({{cog|da|mandlig}}, {{cog|sv|manlig}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmænli/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-manly.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Having the [[characteristic]]s of a [[man]].
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|1|II|vi|page=39|what God or Feend, or ſpirit of the earth,<br>Or Monſter turned to a '''manly''' ſhape,<br>Or of what mould or mettel he be made,<br>What ſtar or ſtate ſoeuer gouern him,<br>Let vs put on our meet incountering mindes, {{...}}}}
# Having qualities viewed as befitting a man; [[courageous]], [[resolute]], [[noble]].
#*{{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|II|iii|passage=Let's briefly put on '''manly''' readiness.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|15|passage=Serene and '''manly''', harden'd to sustain / The load of life.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2001|author=Thomas W. Smith|title=Revaluing Ethics: Aristotle's Dialectical Pedagogy|page=86
|passage=Without a successful defense of one's city, none of the other virtues would be possible; '''manly''' courage seems to be a precondition for anything else worth achieving in life.}}

